<%# One funding rule. Local: `rule` (BudgetPagePresenter::Rule).

    The date is printed only where the rule has one. An anchorless rate rule is never "due", so
    `#next_due_on` is nil there and the row stays quiet about dates.

    THE REASON CLAUSE IS GONE WITH THE ORPHANS (two-ledger spec §5). It said why a rule was outside
    the fill order; every rule this page renders is owned by a category.

    ** §3.4'S ROW (Task 3), REPLACING TASK 2'S MINIMUM. ** That task left `· $250.00 claimed` /
    `· $450.00 built up` here and named this as the replacement — REPLACING them rather than standing
    beside them, or the row would say the same money twice. What the row prints now is §3.4 verbatim:

      RATE      $310.00 of $400.00                          spent of this period's rate, over in red
      DATED     $450.00 built up of $1,200.00                the running total against the bill,
                next due Mar 1 · $200.00 per period            with the schedule beneath it
      BUILDING  $450.00 built up of $5,000.00                the running total against the cap, and
                +$200.00 per period                            what each period adds; uncapped, the
                                                              figure is `$450.00 built up` alone

    AND IT IS THE SAME SENTENCE HOME PRINTS. `claim_figure` and `claim_schedule` render this row and
    `HomePresenter::ClaimLine` alike — the two screens say one thing about one rule rather than two
    that have to be kept in step. Every figure comes off the page's ONE `ClaimLedger`.

    THE STICKER STAYS BESIDE IT, AND THE REPETITION IS DELIBERATE. `budget_rule_amount` prints what
    the rule SAYS ("$1,200.00 every 6 months") — the thing the Edit link edits — while §3.4's figure
    prints where it has got to. On a dated rule the target IS the sticker, so the same number appears
    twice; that is the declaration confirmed by the reading, not two figures a reader could add. This
    is the page where rules are written, and a page for editing a rule that does not show the rule
    would be the wrong subtraction. %>
<%# STACKS BELOW `sm` (design review H5): the name, "$1,200.00 every 6 months", "· next Mar 1" and
    an Edit link are four things, and at 375px the figure and its date wrapped into the name's
    column. %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-rule="<%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>">
  <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:items-baseline gap-1 sm:gap-4">
    <%# ** THE TYPE, AS A SMALL LABEL BESIDE THE NAME (rules-own-the-budget spec §3). ** The overview
        at the top of the page states the three SUMS; this is the row saying which one it is counted
        in, so a user reading "Choice $300.00 a period" up there can find the three rules that make
        it. It is also the give-way rank made visible — the strip that names who goes without when
        the money runs short reads exactly this word first.

        QUIET TREATMENT, DELIBERATELY: the same grey chip for all three, no colour vocabulary. A red
        "Bill" would read as trouble, and a bill being a bill is not news — every rule has a type and
        the label is a classification, not a state. Trouble on this row is `data-rule-trouble`
        underneath, in the one red this page uses. %>
    <span class="flex items-baseline gap-2 min-w-0">
      <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">
        <%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>
      </span>
      <span class="shrink-0 rounded bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs text-gray-600" data-rule-type="<%= rule.rule_type %>">
        <%= rule_type_label(rule.rule_type) %>
      </span>
    </span>
    <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-2 shrink-0">
      <span><%= budget_rule_amount(rule.budget) %></span>
      <span class="<%= rule.over? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-500" %>" data-rule-figure>
        · <%= claim_figure(rule) %>
      </span>
      <% if schedule = claim_schedule(rule) %>
        <span class="text-gray-500" data-rule-schedule>· <%= schedule %></span>
      <% end %>
      <%= link_to "Edit", edit_budget_path(rule.budget), class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline" %>
    </span>
  </div>

  <%# WHAT IS WRONG, WHERE ANYTHING IS — the same string Home's strip prints about this rule, off the
      same helper, so a user moving between the two screens meets one sentence rather than two. %>
  <% if rule.trouble? %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger font-medium" data-rule-trouble>
      <%= claim_trouble_label(rule) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "adjust", rule: rule %>

  <%# WHAT WAS ALREADY DONE TO THIS PERIOD, in date order, each with the door back out. The claim
      above already counts every one of them — the list is what makes that figure explicable rather
      than merely asserted, which is the whole argument for a dated delta over a silent override.

      Silent when there are none: an empty list under every row would be a heading about nothing on
      the widest per-rule screen in the app. %>
  <% if rule.adjustments.any? %>
    <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1" data-rule-changes>
      <% rule.adjustments.each do |change| %>
        <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 text-xs text-gray-600" data-change="<%= change.id %>">
          <span class="text-gray-500"><%= change.local_day.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
          <%# SIGNED, and `number_to_currency` prints a leading minus for a take-back — which is the
              whole vocabulary of this table said in one character. %>
          <span class="<%= change.amount.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>" data-change-amount>
            <%= number_to_currency(change.amount) %>
          </span>
          <%= button_to "Remove",
                        adjustment_path(change),
                        method: :delete,
                        form: { draggable: "false" },
                        class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline" %>
        </li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  <% end %>
</div>
